EXT. FRONTIER LAUNDRY YARD - HIGH NOON

Wet sheets snap on lines between cottonwood posts. Lye barrels. A wash-tub steams in the sun. Chickens scatter.

Five people stand in a stiff circle in the yard. MARSHAL EZRA OKORO-BELL, tall, black hat, hand loose by his holster. WIDOW HEPZIBAH LINDQVIST-ASANTE, apron, sleeves rolled, a washboard in both fists. The RANCHER BARNABY PRESCOTT-NGUYEN, barrel chest, spurs ringing. His SON, young JEDEDIAH PRESCOTT-NGUYEN, thin, a pistol he doesn't know how to hold. And the preacher TOBIAS ADEYEMI-HOLM, a Bible in one hand and a sagging hat in the other.

Marshal Ezra takes one step forward. Barnaby takes one step back. Hepzibah plants the washboard in the dirt like a flag. Jedediah's gun hand trembles, and the preacher raises his Bible as if to stop a bullet with it, while a sheet billows between them and the sun.

No one speaks. A rooster crows. A bucket tips. Water spreads across the dust.

The sheet drops. Behind it, the four stand exactly where they were. Only Hepzibah has moved: the washboard is now a few inches higher, and she has not blinked.

Barnaby's eyes flick to Jedediah. The boy's gun slowly lowers. The Marshal lets out a very small breath. The preacher puts his hat back on.
